Binary files
description: opaque bytes, read lazily - meaning from the format, decoding from the concrete
- >
  `file/binary` is opaque bytes - an image, a pdf, a djvu, an unknown or large blob: read
  lazily, never parsed as yamlover data. What the bytes *mean* is the format's job -
  `image/png` marks an image, `application/pdf` a document - and how they *decode* into a value
  is the concrete's ([the split](*::meta)): `binary/int32/le` reads a four-byte file as an
  integer, `base64` reads text as bytes (the `!!binary` tag is its inline alias).
- >
  So even a SCALAR may live as raw bytes: a member whose `.yo/meta.yo` clause gives the
  decoding - `concrete: binary/int32/le` turns a four-byte file into an integer
  (examples/55-meta); `type: binary` with `format: image/png` keeps an image an
  image. The data is the on-disk file; the metadata says how to read it - the instance and its
  description never trade places ([metadata first](*::language:principles:metadata-first)). The
  old spelling of a codec as a format (`format: int32/le`) reads forever but is not authored
  anymore.
